74
00:06:18,433 --> 00:06:22,655
Well, I'm going to start out with a bit of news about an extremely old weasel.

75
00:06:22,935 --> 00:06:23,275
Cool.

76
00:06:23,364 --> 00:06:24,945
Or a weasel-like thing.

77
00:06:25,385 --> 00:06:31,749
This is research by Ryan Patterson et al. in the journal The Zoological Journal of the Linnean Society.

78
00:06:32,409 --> 00:06:35,491
And we'll link to an article at CBC News by Emily Chung.

79
00:06:36,391 --> 00:06:44,996
One of the reasons that I was clued into this bit of news is that one of the authors on this paper is our friend Dr. Josh Samuels.

80
00:06:45,456 --> 00:06:45,977
Oh, hey!

81
00:06:46,306 --> 00:06:49,467
Who is currently one of the curators at the Gray Fossil Site.

82
00:06:50,027 --> 00:06:50,508
Cool!

83
00:06:50,628 --> 00:06:52,588
Yeah, Josh is a cool guy.

84
00:06:53,569 --> 00:07:04,134
Josh was formerly working over at the John Day Fossil Beds, which is over in Oregon, which is where this fossil is from, which is when he got involved in this research.

85
00:07:04,494 --> 00:07:08,015
So I said weasel-like things, and what I'm talking about are mastellids.

86
00:07:08,947 --> 00:07:14,329
Now, I don't know how many times we have said the word "mastellid" on the podcast.

87
00:07:14,489 --> 00:07:16,530
I'm sure it's come up a few times.

88
00:07:16,550 --> 00:07:17,730
I don't think it's very many.

89
00:07:18,250 --> 00:07:24,193
Mastellids are the group of carnivorans, which means that they are cousins of cats and dogs and bears and such.

90
00:07:24,913 --> 00:07:28,735
The mastellids include weasels and ferrets and weasel-like things.

91
00:07:28,935 --> 00:07:29,275
Otters.

92
00:07:29,463 --> 00:07:38,088
Otters, wolverines, a lot of that sort of small-ish, usually carnivorous, mammals.

93
00:07:38,108 --> 00:07:40,709
Yeah, often long-bodied and short-limbed.

94
00:07:41,226 --> 00:07:56,132
Fossil mastellids are found in a variety of places, but the oldest mastellid fossils are in Europe, dating back to the Middle Oligocene, which seems to suggest that that's probably where they got their start.

95
00:07:56,592 --> 00:08:00,294
The mastellid fossil record seems to originate in Europe.

96
00:08:01,027 --> 00:08:10,671
Here in North America, there are lots of mastellid fossils from the Miocene toward the present, but very few before that.

97
00:08:11,451 --> 00:08:11,731
Okay.

98
00:08:12,231 --> 00:08:24,135
And when they are before that, it's around the sort of late Oligocene into the early Miocene time period, so we're looking at around 25 million years ago or so.

99
00:08:24,724 --> 00:08:34,947
This new study describes a new mastellid that is slightly older than that, making it the oldest mastellid in North America.

100
00:08:35,347 --> 00:08:35,587
Neat.

101
00:08:35,963 --> 00:09:03,810
It is described from a nearly complete cranium, which means the upper part of the skull, like excluding the lower jaw, and part of the lower jaw, identified as a new genus and species, Coromictus walsinae, from the John Day Formation in Oregon, known for the famous John Day Fossil Beds National Monument, an actual really cool place to go, which I have not been.

102
00:09:04,348 --> 00:09:20,773
Dating to somewhere between roughly 29 and roughly 26 million years old, which means that this is at least 1 million years older than the previous oldest mastellid here in North America.

103
00:09:21,130 --> 00:09:39,695
The fossil itself was discovered about 15 years ago, and according to the article, the CBC News article, originally identified as some sort of ancient feline, or cat-like thing, and put on a display at a local museum, which is, the story goes, where Josh noticed it.

104
00:09:41,115 --> 00:09:45,636
I have not actually asked Josh to confirm this, but this is what it says in the news story.

105
00:09:46,168 --> 00:09:52,590
And so he got the colleagues involved, and they put together this paper describing it.

106
00:09:53,050 --> 00:10:02,113
Not only is it a noticeably old mastellid, it's also the first mastellid in that region.

107
00:10:02,493 --> 00:10:02,853
Okay.

108
00:10:02,864 --> 00:10:13,208
So that fossil depositional region over there, there are lots of mammal fossils discovered there, but this is the first mastellid from that area as well.

109
00:10:13,788 --> 00:10:25,492
Anatomically, it shows some features that form a bit of an evolutionary bridge between the early European forms and the later North American forms.

110
00:10:25,588 --> 00:10:37,472
So some of the oldest - I read in the paper - some of the oldest mastellid fossils in North America belong to a group called the oligoboninae, which makes me think of bunnies, but I'm sure is not.

111
00:10:39,393 --> 00:10:44,515
So it is an early mastellid, it is an unusual find, not just for its time, but for its place.

112
00:10:45,168 --> 00:10:50,790
It forms a bit of a link between the older ones and then the shift over to North America.

113
00:10:51,710 --> 00:10:59,052
And they also made note that its size seems to fit a certain ecological trend.

114
00:10:59,593 --> 00:11:00,473
It is very small.

115
00:11:01,006 --> 00:11:01,366
Okay.

116
00:11:01,386 --> 00:11:13,612
The skull is about four centimeters long, so it's not ridiculously small for a mastellid, but it's on the smaller side of the sort of spectrum of mastellids.

117
00:11:14,249 --> 00:11:18,611
And the authors suggest that that might be related to environmental changes.

118
00:11:19,146 --> 00:11:27,549
So there is reason to think, based on other studies, that as things get drier, animals like this might get smaller.

119
00:11:28,289 --> 00:11:34,351
This was a time that you have increasing dryness, grasslands taking over where once there were forests.

120
00:11:35,411 --> 00:11:41,973
And it is also a time in the fossil record where we see burrowing rodents becoming a lot more common.

121
00:11:43,607 --> 00:11:48,089
And today, if you look at animals like weasels, those are a preferred prey.

122
00:11:48,490 --> 00:12:01,675
So small size may have been related to that changing in environments, and maybe even their size may have been related to an abundance of prey that might have required you to go chasing things into burrows to get after.

123
00:12:02,135 --> 00:12:06,777
It's fun whenever we get to, you know, I guess this technically, yeah, this would be a range extension.

124
00:12:07,128 --> 00:12:09,529
It is a temporal and spatial range extension.

125
00:12:09,569 --> 00:12:10,990
Yeah, those are always fun.

126
00:12:11,470 --> 00:12:15,552
And we don't talk a lot about mastellids, little carnivorous mammals.

127
00:12:15,712 --> 00:12:22,074
It's weird. Mastellids are, many in that group, are very well known animals.

128
00:12:22,094 --> 00:12:25,475
Like everyone knows what a weasel is. Everyone knows what an otter is.

129
00:12:25,836 --> 00:12:28,757
But it's very rare that they're talked about as a group, I feel.

130
00:12:29,097 --> 00:12:31,158
Yeah. Well, they're very diverse.

131
00:12:31,845 --> 00:12:34,206
They're kind of, in my head, they feel like rodents.

132
00:12:34,586 --> 00:12:34,986
Yeah.

133
00:12:35,187 --> 00:12:40,710
Where it's like, that is a word that describes a vast diversity of small animals.

134
00:12:41,370 --> 00:12:44,051
And I think of one or two, but I know there's a bunch more.

135
00:12:44,211 --> 00:12:44,372
Yeah.

136
00:12:44,992 --> 00:12:47,373
And so it's like when I said weasel or weasel-like thing.

137
00:12:47,933 --> 00:12:50,575
It's the kind of group that's very difficult to sum up.

138
00:12:50,955 --> 00:12:54,037
Yeah, they don't have one feature that is their thing.

139
00:12:54,049 --> 00:12:54,570
Exactly.

140
00:12:54,590 --> 00:12:56,973
Like a badger is not an otter.

141
00:12:57,364 --> 00:12:57,624
No.

142
00:12:59,125 --> 00:13:00,906
It's not like bear. Bear is pretty much a bear.

143
00:13:00,946 --> 00:13:01,166
Yes.

144
00:13:02,027 --> 00:13:06,129
So yeah, way to go Josh, and all, and colleagues.

145
00:13:06,929 --> 00:13:13,953
Well, speaking of things reacting to climate shifts, this next study deals with ancient fish

146
00:13:14,526 --> 00:13:21,288
during a time when the world was cooling at the Eocene/Ologocene boundary.

147
00:13:21,668 --> 00:13:25,910
Okay, so one epoch earlier than this mustelid.

148
00:13:26,230 --> 00:13:30,352
And so this is looking at how sea life basically reacted,

149
00:13:30,412 --> 00:13:36,114
specifically fish reacted to that cooling event around Antarctica.

150
00:13:36,284 --> 00:13:41,206
This is research by Elizabeth Sivert et al. in Nature Geoscience.

151
00:13:41,846 --> 00:13:48,629
And the article is also by Elizabeth Sivert in the Nature Research, Ecology, and Evolution Behind the Paper.

152
00:13:49,089 --> 00:13:49,889
Oh, that's cool.

153
00:13:49,929 --> 00:13:56,012
It's a portion written by Elizabeth, and then an interview with the thoughts from.

154
00:13:56,092 --> 00:13:57,172
Oh, that's fun.

155
00:13:57,492 --> 00:13:59,773
And so it's a good article.

156
00:14:00,083 --> 00:14:08,887
So this is dealing with a time at that Oligocene/Eocene boundary, a point where things started to cool down aggressively.

157
00:14:09,188 --> 00:14:16,891
Atmospheric CO2 started to decline, and as it hit a low threshold, the seasonal snows around Antarctica

158
00:14:17,172 --> 00:14:24,155
became year-round and started sticking, which gave us the annual ice caps that are there all year.

159
00:14:24,486 --> 00:14:29,668
Yeah, this was the time where we're transitioning from what they call greenhouse conditions to icehouse conditions.

160
00:14:30,209 --> 00:14:32,089
The world we live in now with permanent ice caps.

161
00:14:32,409 --> 00:14:42,393
This was about 34 million years ago, and it triggered a number of environmental shifts, especially in the ocean.

162
00:14:42,826 --> 00:14:50,349
So as things got colder, there was an obvious shift in plankton communities, carbon cycling,

163
00:14:50,929 --> 00:14:59,152
and there's lots of evidence showing that this had an effect on ancient whales at the time, increasing their diversification.

164
00:14:59,607 --> 00:15:07,930
So trying to investigate this seeming connection between the climactic shift and the base of the food web in the ocean,

165
00:15:08,390 --> 00:15:18,454
so they looked at fish from that time, hypothesizing that this climactic shift would show a significant change

166
00:15:18,967 --> 00:15:26,630
in the abundance and diversity, so amount and amount of types of fish, in response to the food web shifting.

167
00:15:26,950 --> 00:15:34,493
Up or down, they weren't hypothesizing some kind of change, but that surely, if we're changing the base of the food web,

168
00:15:34,753 --> 00:15:36,834
we're going to see the fish adjust to it.

169
00:15:37,194 --> 00:15:43,096
So they went looking for fish teeth, because as common as fish are, the most diverse of vertebrate groups,

170
00:15:43,825 --> 00:15:49,067
they can be kind of rare in the fossil record, because they're delicate skeletons, and if you're dying out in the ocean,

171
00:15:49,087 --> 00:15:54,049
there's no guarantee that you've settled anywhere that's going to fossilize.

172
00:15:54,569 --> 00:15:58,530
But their teeth tend to fossilize much better, because it's tougher than their skeleton.

173
00:15:58,851 --> 00:16:04,113
And they searched for them with the help of the International Ocean Discovery Program,

174
00:16:04,686 --> 00:16:10,769
which is an organization that does drilling, core sampling of the seafloor.

175
00:16:11,109 --> 00:16:20,833
They sorted through 717 chunks of deep sea core samples from seven different sites around the world,

176
00:16:21,128 --> 00:16:29,111
ranging between 28 to 42 million years old, and they picked out about 50,623 fish teeth.

177
00:16:29,191 --> 00:16:33,493
Wow. That is quite a study.

178
00:16:34,894 --> 00:16:37,475
They picked out every tooth they were able to find.

179
00:16:37,895 --> 00:16:41,396
Wow. Looking at this, they first looked at the biomass.

180
00:16:41,845 --> 00:16:46,527
So the fish production of that time actually seems to be constant.

181
00:16:46,807 --> 00:16:47,968
Okay, so not changing.

182
00:16:48,028 --> 00:16:52,410
So we don't see more fish being born and populations increasing or decreasing.

183
00:16:52,710 --> 00:16:56,812
They seem to not respond at all to the climate shift.

184
00:16:57,712 --> 00:17:00,974
So fish numbers don't seem to be adjusting, so they looked at diversity.

185
00:17:01,224 --> 00:17:09,748
They sorted through and then grouped them by morphology so that they could start to get an idea of the different types.

186
00:17:10,268 --> 00:17:11,849
How many different shapes are we seeing?

187
00:17:11,869 --> 00:17:20,353
Yeah, we can't say which species and how many species for sure, but look at if that diversity increases or declines during that time.

188
00:17:20,706 --> 00:17:32,591
And what they found was a fairly constant increase in the morphotype diversity throughout that 14 million years that the range was looking at,

189
00:17:32,951 --> 00:17:35,272
which means there were no spikes or major dips.

190
00:17:35,773 --> 00:17:38,774
It was just a steady increase in diversity.

191
00:17:38,974 --> 00:17:40,295
So once again, no reaction.

192
00:17:40,355 --> 00:17:42,776
A slow pattern that's not responding to that climate.

193
00:17:42,963 --> 00:17:46,385
Yeah, we don't see any anomaly in the pattern.

194
00:17:46,745 --> 00:17:49,246
As the author put it, they were wrong on all counts.

195
00:17:50,486 --> 00:17:55,309
Both the abundance and diversity were stable across this major climactic shift.

196
00:17:55,925 --> 00:18:10,551
So they took a little bit of a deeper look and looked at molecular phylogenies and ecological and geological proxies to compile a massive look at the ecosystem during this time.

197
00:18:11,011 --> 00:18:19,214
And while a lot of the papers saw increases or decreases in the primary productivity during that time in the ocean,

198
00:18:19,628 --> 00:18:25,890
most regions don't record any long-term shifts and are actually fairly in line with the fish.

199
00:18:26,370 --> 00:18:32,512
And so what it actually seems like is that a lot of the things did not happen during that shift.

200
00:18:32,572 --> 00:18:40,354
For instance, the Antarctic krill and baleen-bearing whales, so the filter-feeding whales, didn't evolve until after.

201
00:18:40,794 --> 00:18:44,396
So they did not respond to that shift during the shift.

202
00:18:44,805 --> 00:18:46,746
It was a good bit after.

203
00:18:47,446 --> 00:18:57,369
What this shows is that the ocean ecosystem does not seem to respond to this major global shift in the way they expected,

204
00:18:57,950 --> 00:19:03,392
and that some of the shifts that are attributed to it happened at later times or different times.

205
00:19:03,887 --> 00:19:14,751
So basically it's just revealing that our understanding of that shift and the causes it had, or the effects it had on the ecosystem, is not as clear-cut as was expected.

206
00:19:15,051 --> 00:19:25,935
So they went looking for answers in one place, didn't find what they expected, and then went looking at other sources of data and found that those were also not quite what they expected.

207
00:19:26,408 --> 00:19:30,089
And then perhaps we have to rethink how we understand this big change.

208
00:19:30,229 --> 00:19:37,851
Yeah, and so it's one of those perfect examples of they formed a hypothesis that was completely wrong,

209
00:19:39,052 --> 00:19:49,255
and has led them to slightly change their view of this major event and realize they really need to look into it more closely.

210
00:19:49,687 --> 00:19:58,831
It's always fun when we find a study that looks for the sign of something in one source of data and doesn't see it.

211
00:19:59,151 --> 00:20:05,293
Like that if you had just studied fish, at least based on their findings, you wouldn't know that anything had changed.

212
00:20:05,313 --> 00:20:12,295
Yeah, if you were just looking at fish during this time frame, you would have no clue that there was a climactic shift.

213
00:20:12,866 --> 00:20:18,108
So it reinforces the importance of combining as many different studies as you can.

214
00:20:18,628 --> 00:20:24,570
And then reviewing them often to make sure that they're actually saying what we think they're saying.

215
00:20:24,830 --> 00:20:26,031
Well that's pretty cool.

216
00:20:26,471 --> 00:20:28,972
Well my next bit of news is also in the ocean.

217
00:20:29,524 --> 00:20:43,751
Somewhat older, we're going back to the late Cretaceous to a study on bivalve growth patterns that gets down to a surprisingly detailed level of resolution.

218
00:20:44,512 --> 00:20:50,515
This is research by Niels de Winter et al. in the journal Paleoceanography and Paleoclimatology.

219
00:20:50,967 --> 00:20:54,548
And we'll link to an article by Megan Bartels in space.com.

220
00:20:55,308 --> 00:20:57,629
You may be wondering why space.com. Just hang on.

221
00:20:58,249 --> 00:21:03,891
This research focused on a shell of a rudest bivalve.

222
00:21:03,991 --> 00:21:06,732
So bivalves are your clams, oysters, etc.

223
00:21:07,352 --> 00:21:13,994
Rudests, we mentioned them in episode 36 about reefs, because they were, in the Mesozoic, reef-building bivalves.

224
00:21:14,554 --> 00:21:16,255
I don't know that all of them were reef builders.

225
00:21:16,845 --> 00:21:20,467
This one is named Taurites sanchezi.

226
00:21:21,307 --> 00:21:32,172
The shell was discovered in a Campanian deposit, so late Cretaceous in the range of 80 to 70 million years ago, in Oman on the Arabian Peninsula.

227
00:21:32,712 --> 00:21:39,015
What they were looking at is the chemical shifts through the shell.

228
00:21:39,367 --> 00:21:45,649
So as the bivalve grew, it added layer upon layer upon layer to the shell, kind of like tree rings.

229
00:21:46,149 --> 00:21:48,870
Or bones, depending on what bony animal you're looking at.

230
00:21:49,450 --> 00:21:57,033
And as the shell grew, they could see those growth layers revealed through chemical analysis.

231
00:21:57,466 --> 00:22:05,069
So they were looking at trace elements, they were looking at stable isotopes of things like magnesium and calcium and strontium and lithium, they listed a few.

232
00:22:05,569 --> 00:22:15,574
And what they were able to see is the sort of cycles of growth, which, as you might expect, reveal seasonal changes.

233
00:22:16,034 --> 00:22:18,995
So you can see sort of winter to summer, dry to wet or whatever.

234
00:22:19,095 --> 00:22:21,576
I guess there's no dry to wet in the ocean, but you know what I mean.

235
00:22:22,303 --> 00:22:26,845
But at the even closer level, they could see daily growth lines.

236
00:22:27,746 --> 00:22:28,466
Cool.

237
00:22:28,666 --> 00:22:32,908
The daily layers could be about 40 micrometers thick.

238
00:22:33,688 --> 00:22:37,230
So 40 millionths of a meter. Very, very, very tiny.

239
00:22:37,770 --> 00:22:45,373
This level of detail allowed them to compare daily shifts to seasonal shifts, daily cycles to seasonal cycles.

240
00:22:46,105 --> 00:22:54,568
And one of the things that they found that was very interesting is that the amount of variation from day to day,

241
00:22:54,668 --> 00:23:00,290
so sort of day/night variation in chemistry, was greater than the seasonal variation.

242
00:23:00,971 --> 00:23:01,091
Oh.

243
00:23:01,624 --> 00:23:11,027
So the chemical signatures in the shell as it was growing changed more dramatically from day to night than it did from, say, winter to summer.

244
00:23:11,327 --> 00:23:25,213
And they suggest that this means that temperature changes from season to season were not as important for the growth of the shell as light changes from day to night,

245
00:23:26,028 --> 00:23:39,992
which suggests that these bivalves may have had photosymbionts, photosynthesizing bacteria that lived in and on and amongst the bivalve that helped you feed them, like corals do today.

246
00:23:40,012 --> 00:23:41,432
Yeah, zooxanthellae kind of stuff.

247
00:23:41,632 --> 00:23:42,193
Exactly.

248
00:23:42,402 --> 00:23:53,686
This is also supported, they claim, by the carbon and oxygen isotope signatures in the shell, which are similar to animals like coral and foraminifera that have photosymbionts,

249
00:23:54,087 --> 00:24:04,971
and that they're seeing similar seasonal variation in those signatures that would be impacted by your photosymbiotic bacteria and such.

250
00:24:05,787 --> 00:24:12,829
So number one, they have potentially revealed a photosymbiotic habit in these ancient bivalves,

251
00:24:13,430 --> 00:24:25,013
and they point out that since bivalve shells preserve really well, this approach they're using, using multiple different chemical signatures to study these variations throughout the year,

252
00:24:25,468 --> 00:24:35,191
might be a good approach to examining environmental changes in the past, perhaps even inferring sunlight intensity in environments,

253
00:24:36,052 --> 00:24:47,576
and, and this is my favorite line in their paper, that being able to compare daily to seasonal cycles might bridge the gap between climate and weather reconstructions in the past.

254
00:24:47,896 --> 00:24:48,716
Wow.

255
00:24:48,727 --> 00:24:53,809
Because if you can look at a daily environmental signature, you can see weather, potentially.

256
00:24:54,009 --> 00:24:57,310
Those are all really awesome things to learn.

257
00:24:58,591 --> 00:25:09,676
If they were photosynthetic, you know, in some regard, that's particularly interesting to me because that's another photosynthetic coral builder, or reef builder.

258
00:25:10,016 --> 00:25:11,136
And it makes sense, like.

259
00:25:11,526 --> 00:25:14,347
Right. If you want to be a reef builder, apparently it's a good way to do it.

260
00:25:14,367 --> 00:25:18,508
If you're going to build an underwater rainforest, yeah.

261
00:25:19,109 --> 00:25:26,391
And even if you're not, even if you're a foram floating around, there is of course one other thing they were able to note that will make Will very happy,

262
00:25:27,091 --> 00:25:33,794
and is the reason that it was, this article is at space.com, they were also able to count how many days there were in a year.

263
00:25:33,934 --> 00:25:34,934
Yeah.

264
00:25:34,965 --> 00:25:41,149
Because you can compare seasonal shifts, which is a year, and a year has not changed.

265
00:25:42,029 --> 00:25:49,293
Ask an astronomer, the amount of time it takes the earth to go around the sun has been a constant for the history of the planet.

266
00:25:49,573 --> 00:25:58,417
But the length of a day has not. They counted 372 daily layers per year.

267
00:25:58,457 --> 00:25:59,618
Because days were shorter.

268
00:25:59,665 --> 00:26:00,966
Because days were shorter.

269
00:26:00,986 --> 00:26:02,627
Because we were spinning faster.

270
00:26:02,747 --> 00:26:04,027
Because we were spinning faster.

271
00:26:04,067 --> 00:26:07,409
Because we hadn't had a moon as long as we've had a moon.

272
00:26:07,729 --> 00:26:11,650
Yes, the moon is slowly robbing the earth of its rotational speed.

273
00:26:11,670 --> 00:26:20,074
It is acting as a gravitational air brake, pulling against the earth as we spin, and we are slowing down bit by bit.

274
00:26:20,114 --> 00:26:23,155
And the moon is using this extra gravitational energy to escape.

275
00:26:23,235 --> 00:26:23,436
Yes.

276
00:26:23,923 --> 00:26:30,946
It's slowly moving away from us. Because the moon is speeding up its orbit while we are slowing down our rotation, and so we are losing the moon.

277
00:26:31,186 --> 00:26:31,386
Yep.

278
00:26:31,546 --> 00:26:34,268
By like an inch and a half a year or something like that.

279
00:26:34,568 --> 00:26:40,691
Well, they were able to calculate how many hours were in the day. So this is 80 to 70 million years ago.

280
00:26:41,411 --> 00:26:43,232
23 hours and 31 minutes.

281
00:26:43,952 --> 00:26:45,673
That's fantastic.

282
00:26:45,693 --> 00:26:47,594
The day was a half hour shorter.

283
00:26:47,882 --> 00:26:53,124
Now, this is cool because it, one, matches astronomical models.

284
00:26:53,224 --> 00:26:57,806
Astronomers predict very similar results based on calculating space movements.

285
00:26:58,366 --> 00:27:00,246
Also, this kind of study has been done before.

286
00:27:01,127 --> 00:27:12,330
I know I read a study about Silurian corals, or Devonian maybe, that calculated a time where there were 400 days in a year, or thereabouts, using very similar methods.

287
00:27:12,786 --> 00:27:20,909
And so we have fossil evidence that corroborates astronomical predictions, which is one of my favorite things in the fossil record.

288
00:27:21,368 --> 00:27:39,774
And finally, the group points out that because they were able, with this one shell, to study nine years with four different chemical element approaches, this is a great way to test those astronomical predictions.

289
00:27:40,269 --> 00:27:47,111
Because you're basically doing several different tests, corroborating each other as you go.

290
00:27:47,311 --> 00:27:56,594
It's always easy to forget that what happens out in space and the behavior of our planet vastly affects life on Earth.

291
00:27:56,934 --> 00:27:57,274
Yeah.

