00:29:07,923 --> 00:29:23,591
Troglomorphic is features that you typically see in many cave dwelling species, which include reduced eyes, extended limbs, extended antennae, smaller wings, pale bodies.

314
00:29:24,211 --> 00:29:26,532
You're not having to protect yourself from sunlight anymore.

315
00:29:26,967 --> 00:29:38,950
These are common features in many, many insects, but also vertebrates and other crustaceans and fish that we find in a cave.

316
00:29:38,970 --> 00:29:39,531
Salamanders.

317
00:29:39,551 --> 00:29:39,771
Oh, yeah.

318
00:29:39,811 --> 00:29:40,791
Cave stuff.

319
00:29:41,351 --> 00:29:49,794
Basically, any animal that has adapted to live permanently in a cave often shows some of these features, minus the antennae and wings if they don't have those.

320
00:29:50,625 --> 00:29:59,248
But it is unusual to find in the fossil record because these things are living in caves and are not always preserved.

321
00:29:59,348 --> 00:30:08,272
Even though caves are often good for preserving fossils, many cave fossils are not animals living in the cave, but visiting the cave.

322
00:30:08,852 --> 00:30:14,494
You know, things using it as a layer or a den, you know, a temporary den or that got trapped.

323
00:30:14,826 --> 00:30:23,129
So because of this, Troglomorphic, you know, animals, fossils from caves are not well known before the Cenozoic.

324
00:30:23,789 --> 00:30:27,410
So before the end of the age of the dinosaurs.

325
00:30:27,911 --> 00:30:30,231
We don't have many from that time.

326
00:30:30,565 --> 00:30:46,252
Almost all of it since then, and this has led to questions about when exactly did many of these groups evolve because a lot of data shows that they evolved earlier than their fossils are found.

327
00:30:46,833 --> 00:30:52,816
And these fossils are now the oldest fossils with these cave features.

328
00:30:53,156 --> 00:30:54,296
That's pretty cool.

329
00:30:54,465 --> 00:30:58,487
So this puts them in the Cretaceous at the end of the age of the dinosaurs.

