00:55:17,445 --> 00:55:17,865
Right, right.

616
00:55:17,925 --> 00:55:25,028
Which is a area where we have a fossil here, but we should have fossils going back to here, according to the other evidence.

617
00:55:25,368 --> 00:55:31,529
This coelacanth was Eoactinistia forii and is currently the oldest coelacanth known.

618
00:55:31,885 --> 00:55:44,469
So coelacanths were at least around and getting their start just over 400 million years ago and were successful for the next 100 to couple hundred million years.

619
00:55:45,108 --> 00:56:05,035
They were fairly diverse, if not in morphology, but in groups. There were a number of different coelacanth groups that were alive during this time. Some like the Styloichthys and the Whiteyidae were two groups that looked like coelacanths, just slightly smaller, slightly different features here and there.

620
00:56:05,930 --> 00:56:13,012
There were a few that kind of stand out. Not a lot, really. Coelacanths are fairly conserved, conservative.

621
00:56:13,032 --> 00:56:14,953
Yeah, they looked a lot like coelacanths.

622
00:56:14,993 --> 00:56:22,515
Yeah, like you can tell a coelacanth when you see a coelacanth because it looks like a coelacanth, just a little bit smaller, maybe bigger fins here or there.

623
00:56:23,335 --> 00:56:30,537
There is one, the Rebelitricidae with the genus Rebelitrix, which is a cool, cool species name.

624
00:56:30,666 --> 00:56:32,607
Pretty sure that fish worked for Voldemort.

625
00:56:32,747 --> 00:56:36,928
Rebelitrix divaricera, the rebel coelacanth with a forked tail.

626
00:56:38,169 --> 00:56:38,689
Cool.

627
00:56:38,729 --> 00:56:46,892
Right? Because this one had very large tail fins. So coelacanth tails are weird in that they're also kind of lobed.

628
00:56:47,912 --> 00:56:52,554
Like when you see a coelacanth tail, there's this big paddle of flesh.

629
00:56:52,674 --> 00:56:53,474
Right, like a mitten.

630
00:56:53,583 --> 00:57:00,206
Like a mitten with fins around the outside and then this little boop of tail fin at the very end of it.

631
00:57:01,027 --> 00:57:08,350
This coelacanth, the mitten had two big like tuna fins coming off of it.

632
00:57:08,370 --> 00:57:08,850
Like a fish.

633
00:57:09,090 --> 00:57:09,730
Like a fish.

634
00:57:09,750 --> 00:57:09,970
Yeah.

635
00:57:10,891 --> 00:57:15,413
Which seems to suggest that this was a fast pursuit predator.

636
00:57:15,453 --> 00:57:16,333
A speedy coelacanth.

637
00:57:16,363 --> 00:57:25,107
That would have been chasing food instead of today's, which just hover and then they can do one big push with the tail to get away or go after food.

638
00:57:25,887 --> 00:57:29,148
So it does seem like some were living different lifestyles.

639
00:57:30,289 --> 00:57:37,012
These would have ranged to about four-ish feet long, so not quite as big as today's, but not small.

640
00:57:37,366 --> 00:57:42,008
There also were both marine and freshwater coelacanths.

641
00:57:42,708 --> 00:57:55,634
One that was found in both of these groups was a family known as the coelacanthidae, which is obviously was named before the ones we have today whose scientific name is not coelacanth.

642
00:57:55,714 --> 00:57:59,796
I was going to say, do ours today not fit in the coelacanthidae?

643
00:57:59,816 --> 00:58:00,816
No, they're not in the coelacanthidae.

644
00:58:00,836 --> 00:58:02,157
That's phenomenal.

645
00:58:02,963 --> 00:58:04,264
That makes me very happy.

646
00:58:04,384 --> 00:58:07,085
They are in the Latimeridae.

647
00:58:07,466 --> 00:58:07,826
Okay.

648
00:58:07,886 --> 00:58:09,207
Latimeridae, the genus.

649
00:58:09,547 --> 00:58:11,688
And so that's where their genus comes from.

650
00:58:12,583 --> 00:58:28,030
But often, especially early on, today's coelacanths were grouped into the coelacanthidae and there's often connections made between those two that are inaccurate now that the family trees or the tree of life has been better filled out for this group.

651
00:58:29,070 --> 00:58:31,511
And then there are a couple of other notable ones.

652
00:58:32,227 --> 00:58:46,731
The megaloselicanthus, which yep, the name right there, these coelacanths were from eastern to central North America, the United States in fact, just about 130 million years ago.

653
00:58:47,171 --> 00:58:49,432
Okay, so early early Cretaceous.

654
00:58:49,472 --> 00:58:49,832
Yep.

655
00:58:50,492 --> 00:58:55,154
And have some of the last known coelacanths in North America.

656
00:58:55,725 --> 00:59:09,132
So the latest ones are found in Europe, but some of the last are found here in the United States are from the megaloselicanthus and they include members that grew up to three and a half meters.

657
00:59:12,573 --> 00:59:14,414
That is a big coelacanth.

658
00:59:14,695 --> 00:59:17,556
It's as large as coelacanths get.

659
00:59:17,596 --> 00:59:19,877
That is an alligator sized coelacanth.

660
00:59:19,904 --> 00:59:20,244
Yes.

661
00:59:21,324 --> 00:59:23,365
An American alligator sized coelacanth.

662
00:59:23,385 --> 00:59:28,647
Yep. So, like, there were some big coelacanths that dwarfed us.

663
00:59:29,688 --> 00:59:36,751
And some of them are swimming around here. There's another one that is in the sub order Latimira oidei.

664
00:59:37,311 --> 00:59:39,512
So we're getting close to today's coelacanths.

665
00:59:40,065 --> 00:59:54,150
But before we get there, we have the mausonia oidei, which has mausonia, a genus that included members that cut up to four meters long, which is the biggest coelacanth.

666
00:59:54,850 --> 00:59:55,530
Wow.

667
00:59:55,570 --> 00:59:56,471
That's as big as they get.

668
00:59:56,711 --> 00:59:57,671
That is a big coelacanth.

669
00:59:57,943 --> 01:00:06,887
These were also Cretaceous, but were found in Africa and they dated just to 99 to 112 million years ago.

670
01:00:07,247 --> 01:00:21,155
Then we get to our coelacanths, the Latimira oidei, which includes one of the last known coelacanths in Europe, I believe, which was the macropoma, which were 70 million years old.

671
01:00:21,715 --> 01:00:23,296
Okay. Right at the end of the Mesozoic.

672
01:00:23,347 --> 01:00:27,429
Right at the end and only grew to be about 22 inches, 55 centimeters.

673
01:00:27,649 --> 01:00:28,789
Oh, a small fish.

674
01:00:28,809 --> 01:00:31,750
Fairly small. There were small coelacanths among these big ones.

675
01:00:32,431 --> 01:00:36,412
And then that's where the fossil record caps off.

676
01:00:37,593 --> 01:00:39,634
Right at the end of the Cretaceous.

677
01:00:39,794 --> 01:00:45,956
So it was assumed for the longest time that the coelacanths went the way of the dinosaurs, so to say.

678
01:00:45,964 --> 01:00:51,386
That they also died out during the K-PG extinction or maybe just before it.

679
01:00:51,646 --> 01:00:54,307
Right, right. So it sounds a lot like trilobites.

680
01:00:54,567 --> 01:00:54,887
Yeah.

681
01:00:55,227 --> 01:01:07,072
In episode 82, you have a big burst of diversity early on and then you slowly on the way declining until you peter out at one of the big five mass extinctions.

682
01:01:08,012 --> 01:01:11,534
So that's where our fossil story ends.

683
01:01:11,866 --> 01:01:18,428
So in 1937, if you had asked somebody, they would have said, "Yeah, that was the story of coelacanths."

684
01:01:18,448 --> 01:01:27,450
That was the story of coelacanths, which gives us a really weird situation in that we have two species of coelacanths nowadays that we don't have a fossil record for.

685
01:01:27,610 --> 01:01:27,830
Yeah.

686
01:01:28,405 --> 01:01:41,891
We don't have a fossil record for these two animals because according to the genetic data on the two species, it seems like their divergence falls somewhere in the 30 to 40 million year range.

687
01:01:42,805 --> 01:01:57,750
So they diverge after that end of the fossil record, so we don't have a fossil record for when did they diverge, what did their direct ancestors look like, how did they change over that time.

688
01:01:58,590 --> 01:02:01,091
So we have to turn to genetics for a lot of these things.

689
01:02:01,709 --> 01:02:15,394
And it does seem that if that data is correct, it would coincide with the collision of India and Eurasia about 50 million years ago, which many think is probably the thing that separated the population.

690
01:02:16,227 --> 01:02:28,571
As tributaries and rivers started pouring sediment into the water between Africa and Indonesia, it separated and they diversified into the two species they speciated.

691
01:02:28,751 --> 01:02:29,291
Makes sense.

692
01:02:29,591 --> 01:02:33,733
But that's, that's really, that's what we have on coelacanths.

693
01:02:33,873 --> 01:02:40,175
It's a early diversity, which is funny because we have what appears to be one of the oldest coelacanths.

694
01:02:40,195 --> 01:02:40,475
Yes.

695
01:02:41,443 --> 01:02:51,986
Diverse through the Paleozoic, declining through the Mesozoic, disappear at the extinction, and then 70 million years later are fished up off the coast of Africa.

696
01:02:52,126 --> 01:02:52,486
Yeah.

697
01:02:53,086 --> 01:02:54,567
So let's talk about that fishing up.

698
01:02:54,827 --> 01:02:55,427
Yeah, let's.

699
01:02:55,507 --> 01:03:00,888
Let's bring it to the modern day now that we know what animal we're talking about and why their story is so weird.

700
01:03:01,343 --> 01:03:09,767
Let's talk about how we found them and what we've been able to learn now that we have living specimens to study after this break.

701
01:03:23,330 --> 01:03:41,295
On December 23rd, 1938, Henrik Goosen, the captain of a trawler named the Nereen, was fishing off the coast of East London, South Africa, and was returning to harbor with the crew's catch.

702
01:03:41,675 --> 01:03:43,936
I feel like I should be making background noises.

703
01:03:43,976 --> 01:03:44,336
Right.

704
01:03:44,386 --> 01:03:45,748
Of like a harbor.

705
01:03:45,928 --> 01:03:46,649
Ding, ding, ding.

706
01:03:46,704 --> 01:03:47,404
Yep, yep.

707
01:03:49,863 --> 01:03:53,104
On that trip, they had caught a weird fish.

708
01:03:53,944 --> 01:04:05,628
And so he telephoned his friend, Marjorie Courtenay Latimer, who is the curator at the East London Museum, phoned her to say, "Hey, I have a weird fish. Would you like to come take a look at it?"

709
01:04:06,509 --> 01:04:08,569
The kind of call that scientists love to get.

710
01:04:08,626 --> 01:04:20,650
Yes. It was something that he had done, you know, that he would often call her down to be like, "Hey, do you want to come look through the catch before we take it to market, see if there's anything weird that is important to you and not as important to us?"

711
01:04:21,285 --> 01:04:35,589
He had set the strange fish aside, had tried to protect it, made the point to the crew to not damage it, and when she got there to look at it, she had no clue what it was and could not find a description of it in any fish book.

712
01:04:35,689 --> 01:04:46,372
There was no ID for this fish. So, she set it aside. She couldn't find any place to store it, so she had to get it taxidermied to preserve it.

713
01:04:46,768 --> 01:04:55,651
But she set it aside and contacted her friend, Professor J. L. B. Smith, who was the only ichthologist in South Africa at that time.

714
01:04:56,111 --> 01:05:10,155
So, once he was finally able to, he was delayed by a trip, I believe, but once he was finally able to get there, he immediately recognized it as a coelacanth from the fossil record and freaked out.

715
01:05:10,368 --> 01:05:11,168
Like you would.

716
01:05:11,748 --> 01:05:26,013
And so, the genus and species were named, and Latimeria was named after its discoverer, or the first person to bring it to science, and it was described the next year in 1939.

717
01:05:26,073 --> 01:05:29,073
So, it got its name the year after it was discovered.

718
01:05:30,394 --> 01:05:32,214
The same year that the Wizard of Oz came out.

719
01:05:32,263 --> 01:05:41,626
Yes, exactly. And was only known at that time from the western Indian Ocean around the Comoros Islands.

720
01:05:42,407 --> 01:05:47,809
And then that was the story of the coelacanth pretty much until 1997.

721
01:05:49,589 --> 01:05:50,089
Really?

722
01:05:50,109 --> 01:05:50,910
1997.

723
01:05:51,310 --> 01:05:55,491
The year that the Lost World Jurassic Park came out.

724
01:05:57,084 --> 01:06:05,607
Yes, I was eight years old when the Indonesian coelacanth was discovered in much the same way.

725
01:06:06,068 --> 01:06:16,932
Nearly 60 years later, and 6,000 miles away from the initial discovery, another coelacanth was captured by another group of fishermen.

726
01:06:17,413 --> 01:06:19,894
Is this the second coelacanth specimen?

727
01:06:20,390 --> 01:06:21,471
This is the second species.

728
01:06:21,531 --> 01:06:30,236
So they were finding some of the African near-species, and it was 60 years before the second species was found.

729
01:06:30,246 --> 01:06:33,607
Yeah, we knew half of the species that were alive.

730
01:06:33,687 --> 01:06:35,408
I mean, half as far as we know.

731
01:06:35,448 --> 01:06:36,088
Yeah, exactly.

732
01:06:36,148 --> 01:06:38,609
Who's to say what'll happen in 2057?

733
01:06:39,489 --> 01:06:43,471
This time, researcher Mark Erdmann was given the specimen to research.

734
01:06:43,884 --> 01:06:48,145
After taking some tissue samples, he donated the fish to the Indonesian Institute of Sciences.

735
01:06:48,786 --> 01:07:05,292
Now, this is where this story gets kind of interesting, because as Erdmann claims, the Institute had agreed that his team at the University of Texas was going to be the first to publish, and then the Institute was going to be able to name it.

736
01:07:05,327 --> 01:07:11,089
So they were going to publish on the research of the DNA to see if it was a new species, and then the Institute would name it.

737
01:07:12,349 --> 01:07:24,173
But after handing over to them, the Institute contacted geneticist Laurent Poyot from the French Institute for Developmental Research to help them do their own analysis.

738
01:07:24,513 --> 01:07:24,813
Oh no.

739
01:07:25,333 --> 01:07:28,874
And so both these groups were analyzing the DNA at the same time.

740
01:07:29,304 --> 01:07:42,571
Poyot submitted the report to Nature, I believe, just after, yes, just after the University of Texas' analysis had arrived at the same journal, it was rejected.

741
01:07:43,231 --> 01:07:45,592
Because it was the same study.

742
01:07:45,612 --> 01:07:46,313
Same study.

743
01:07:46,993 --> 01:07:53,136
So they published in the French Academy of Sciences journal and also named it.

744
01:07:53,603 --> 01:07:55,764
So both publications came out.

745
01:07:56,264 --> 01:07:58,245
Yes, but only one named.

746
01:07:58,625 --> 01:08:03,007
Both were studying the DNA, but Poyot named it.

747
01:08:03,667 --> 01:08:07,989
So Erdmann came out and was pretty irate.

748
01:08:08,349 --> 01:08:09,189
Understandably.

749
01:08:10,205 --> 01:08:31,115
There's also a story of a picture that Poyot's team claimed was taken in 1995, and that they had known about it but weren't able to publish due to, I think it was a shipping error, basically, that they, the sample, the specimen had not reached the museum that they sent it to.

750
01:08:31,428 --> 01:08:37,690
Okay, so that's two years before Erdmann brought the specimen to the Institute's attention.

751
01:08:37,710 --> 01:08:38,210
Yes.

752
01:08:38,230 --> 01:08:40,691
So they claimed that, no, they'd already been studying it.

753
01:08:41,271 --> 01:08:55,395
But when the photograph was analyzed by another researcher, they were pretty much 100% convinced it was a fake photograph of Erdmann's specimen.

754
01:08:56,248 --> 01:08:58,388
Wow, scientific chaos.

755
01:08:59,249 --> 01:09:09,653
So it's kind of weird, especially since the species name Menadoinensis is the one we use. That was the one published by Poyot and...

756
01:09:10,053 --> 01:09:11,573
Right, well, it has priority.

757
01:09:11,633 --> 01:09:12,674
Because it was published.

758
01:09:12,734 --> 01:09:13,434
It got published first.

759
01:09:13,702 --> 01:09:23,507
So it's one of those situations where nothing inherently wrong, other than maybe broken promises, but nothing illegal, nothing...

760
01:09:23,767 --> 01:09:26,808
And then maybe some faked photography and some shadiness.

761
01:09:28,429 --> 01:09:30,550
So yeah, it's a weird origin story.

762
01:09:30,670 --> 01:09:33,731
I like that the coelacanth is just full of drama.

763
01:09:33,811 --> 01:09:34,712
Yeah, it is.

764
01:09:34,763 --> 01:09:42,607
This unsuspecting, slow-moving fish just hanging out down deep in the ocean. There's all this drama surrounding it.

765
01:09:42,627 --> 01:09:50,451
Just intrigue. It disappeared and it came back and the way it was discovered is exciting and then there was disagreement and...

766
01:09:52,072 --> 01:09:54,173
Scientific sabotage.

767
01:09:54,186 --> 01:10:00,369
Yes. And the two researchers did disagree a little bit on what they found from the DNA.

768
01:10:00,729 --> 01:10:06,652
Both agreed, yes, it's definitely a distinct species from the West Indian.

769
01:10:07,272 --> 01:10:09,253
Indonesian is a new species.

770
01:10:09,945 --> 01:10:21,029
But the research by Poyot said that their divergence probably was between one to two million years ago, like species-wise.

771
01:10:21,049 --> 01:10:21,509
Very recent.

772
01:10:21,949 --> 01:10:28,372
Yeah, so fairly recent compared to the seven million years ago suggested by Erdmann's research.

773
01:10:28,944 --> 01:10:40,389
So the data there, there's some disagreement with these initial studies, but since then we've now been able to do tons of genetic research.

774
01:10:40,889 --> 01:10:48,172
Yeah, coelacanths have actually been the subject of some pretty prominent and interesting genetic studies.

775
01:10:48,573 --> 01:10:50,053
Their genome has been sequenced.

776
01:10:50,093 --> 01:10:52,534
And there's a couple of reasons why they've had this focus.

777
01:10:53,203 --> 01:11:02,366
So first and foremost, as part of the sarcopterygians, they have a connection to the evolution of land verberates.

778
01:11:02,586 --> 01:11:04,127
Yeah, including us.

779
01:11:04,147 --> 01:11:04,827
Including us.

780
01:11:05,247 --> 01:11:14,050
And that means that not only are they interesting for studying the evolution of our broad group of, you know, our broad section of the animal family tree,

781
01:11:14,746 --> 01:11:26,691
they've also been looked into, and I don't want to jump the gun here, but for developmental studies to understand how our bodies organize themselves.

782
01:11:27,212 --> 01:11:39,177
Absolutely, but there was a debate before we got into all of that, really, that kind of had to be figured out because there's the coelacanths and the lungfish.

783
01:11:39,610 --> 01:11:39,850
Right.

784
01:11:40,310 --> 01:11:43,391
Which one is closest to tetrapods?

785
01:11:43,611 --> 01:11:45,031
Which one has the honor?

786
01:11:45,271 --> 01:11:51,013
Yeah, which one actually is the better representative for early tetrapod evolution?

787
01:11:51,854 --> 01:11:59,756
A lot of people assume the coelacanth because it has the much more obviously formed lobe fins.

788
01:11:59,977 --> 01:12:00,197
Right.

789
01:12:00,524 --> 01:12:07,487
While a lot of lungfish, they do have lobe fins, but they are weird, whip-like tendril things.

790
01:12:07,567 --> 01:12:08,747
Yeah, lungfish are weird.

791
01:12:08,767 --> 01:12:10,348
They're so weird.

792
01:12:10,368 --> 01:12:14,310
Before I said all fish are weird, and then I said coelacanths are real weird, lungfish are real weird.

793
01:12:14,330 --> 01:12:18,731
Yeah, coelacanths have two little arms and two little legs.

794
01:12:19,084 --> 01:12:28,888
Right, if you're comparing, or if you put coelacanths, lungfish, and like a salamander next to each other, yeah, limb-wise, coelacanths looks fitting.

795
01:12:29,328 --> 01:12:40,133
So there's been a lot of debate, no one was quite sure, we didn't have that nice, the earliest fossil record to see that split for sure.

796
01:12:40,693 --> 01:12:44,114
So, in 2013, the genome was sequenced.

797
01:12:44,287 --> 01:12:44,847
Yay!

798
01:12:45,007 --> 01:12:56,772
Now, the genome was sequenced in coelacanths, not the lungfish, and the reason is that coelacanths have a genome that is about 2.8 billion units of DNA,

799
01:12:57,212 --> 01:12:59,713
which is about the same size as the human genome.

800
01:13:00,073 --> 01:13:00,434
Okay.

801
01:13:00,554 --> 01:13:01,134
So decodable.

802
01:13:01,374 --> 01:13:02,134
Yeah, handy.

803
01:13:02,454 --> 01:13:07,497
Lungfish has a genome of 100 billion DNA units.

804
01:13:07,917 --> 01:13:08,517
Why?

805
01:13:08,703 --> 01:13:12,304
Which cannot be sequenced with current technology.

806
01:13:12,584 --> 01:13:13,345
Wow.

807
01:13:13,705 --> 01:13:16,906
So, we sequenced the coelacanth.

808
01:13:17,026 --> 01:13:19,627
For now, and the lungfish can wait.

809
01:13:19,647 --> 01:13:28,431
Yes, and that sequencing did seem to resolve the answer that the lungfish is the closest ancestor to tetrapods.

810
01:13:28,446 --> 01:13:39,271
Comparing to what DNA we had to use of the lungfish, if not the whole genome, that it is coelacanths, then lungfish, then tetrapods, in terms of relatedness.

811
01:13:39,972 --> 01:13:50,557
And so, the coelacanth is not a good proxy, or an exact proxy, for the ancestor to tetrapods, so to speak.

812
01:13:50,989 --> 01:13:54,590
And the lungfish might be, perhaps, closer to that proxy.

813
01:13:54,850 --> 01:13:55,310
Exactly.

814
01:13:55,730 --> 01:14:02,873
But, of the logfinned fish that we have, it's the one we've been able to sequence the genome from.

815
01:14:03,273 --> 01:14:03,553
Right.

816
01:14:04,465 --> 01:14:18,731
So, it has been used as a stand-in for a lot of that research, because it's still developing lobes, it's still developing those primitive limbs, and it still is a close cousin to our closest cousin in the fish group.

817
01:14:18,931 --> 01:14:19,151
Right.

818
01:14:20,732 --> 01:14:25,434
And the cousin of my cousin is a good stand-in while technology progresses.

819
01:14:26,885 --> 01:14:33,569
So, we've done lots of research into features of the coelacanth to look at what it might be able to tell us about tetrapod evolution.

820
01:14:34,129 --> 01:14:36,010
And we've found some weird stuff.

821
01:14:36,190 --> 01:14:36,730
Sure have.

822
01:14:37,671 --> 01:14:41,333
A couple of genes stood out among that sequencing.

823
01:14:42,373 --> 01:14:48,517
One was a gene that is related to those found in animals that develop a placenta.

824
01:14:48,977 --> 01:14:49,057
Oh.

825
01:14:49,392 --> 01:14:49,693
Yeah.

826
01:14:49,873 --> 01:14:52,796
So, the coelacanth does not develop a placenta?

827
01:14:52,843 --> 01:14:53,423
No, it does not.

828
01:14:54,384 --> 01:14:54,624
But it...

829
01:14:54,644 --> 01:15:01,367
But it does produce extremely large eggs with a very good blood supply, and they do hatch within the body.

830
01:15:02,327 --> 01:15:02,747
Okay.

831
01:15:02,907 --> 01:15:14,332
So, this gene may be a kind of an example of what the predecessor gene would have looked like for the one that gave rise to the placenta later on in tetrapods.

832
01:15:14,652 --> 01:15:15,173
Interesting.

833
01:15:15,866 --> 01:15:18,226
And there seems to be some connected features.

834
01:15:18,787 --> 01:15:30,191
You know, it is not growing a placenta, it is not the same gene, but they are doing some weird internal birthing stuff, and they do have a similar gene for that.

835
01:15:30,546 --> 01:15:47,392
But the genes that got most of the press were some limb enhancer genes, BMP7 and GLI3, both of which are critical for the formation of limbs in tetrapods.

836
01:15:47,912 --> 01:15:55,334
And they found them in the coelacanth for very similar purposes, and they are not present in ray-finned fish.

837
01:15:55,388 --> 01:16:02,609
So, these are not found in any other fish group except at least the lobe-finned fish we have sequenced.

838
01:16:03,330 --> 01:16:09,711
And these are genes that activate to drive the growth of limbs in the embryo development.

839
01:16:10,192 --> 01:16:15,553
So, while an embryo is growing, these are the ones that turn on when it is time for the arms and legs to grow.

840
01:16:16,165 --> 01:16:31,471
So, to see how close these genes were to the ones active in tetrapods, they inserted some of these enhancers into the DNA of mice as their embryos were developing, and it made an almost normal limb.

841
01:16:32,211 --> 01:16:39,093
So, these genes from this fish triggered limb development in mice.

842
01:16:39,288 --> 01:16:49,691
Yes, so basically what that's saying is these genes are using the same coding information in a fish as it is in tetrapods like us, at least the mouse.

843
01:16:49,891 --> 01:16:55,893
The mouse genome recognizes that gene as the "start developing your legs" gene.

844
01:16:56,133 --> 01:17:00,895
And it was close enough that it developed a fairly normal mouse limb.

845
01:17:01,285 --> 01:17:08,228
So, this is a really big crack in the case of how did limbs develop.

846
01:17:08,548 --> 01:17:15,230
Right, the foundation of our genetic code for limbs goes all the way back to the lobe-finned fish.

847
01:17:15,551 --> 01:17:23,674
Now, there is a question that this raises, which is why do coelacanths have pseudolims?

848
01:17:24,474 --> 01:17:24,734
Right.

849
01:17:25,025 --> 01:17:28,126
They aren't bottom-dwelling fish that are crawling along the sea floor.

850
01:17:28,546 --> 01:17:37,870
Right, in episode 77 we talked about that question of what potentially drove the development of those lobes in fish,

851
01:17:38,130 --> 01:17:43,733
and we talked about fish crawling around in the shallows or living by the sea floor and pushing themselves along.

852
01:17:44,173 --> 01:17:50,335
But today's coelacanths don't do any of that, like they hover along the walls of mountains.

853
01:17:50,766 --> 01:17:55,528
Right, they're next to the continental shelves or the seamounts and such.

854
01:17:55,908 --> 01:18:00,110
So, they aren't touching a surface almost ever.

855
01:18:00,810 --> 01:18:04,432
So, people have often wondered, and today's coelacanths doesn't make sense,

856
01:18:04,532 --> 01:18:13,135
but there are fossil coelacanths that did live in shallow environments and in estuary environments, so river systems.

857
01:18:14,246 --> 01:18:19,528
So, there were coelacanths that may have been using their limbs in a more limb-like way.

858
01:18:20,148 --> 01:18:22,089
And then the modern ones just held on to it.

859
01:18:22,109 --> 01:18:27,551
Yeah, so it could be that, once again, as we've discussed in other groups, maybe our modern ones are weird.

860
01:18:27,891 --> 01:18:31,092
Yes. Well, again, we talked about this a whole bunch.

861
01:18:31,353 --> 01:18:36,615
Coelacanths are another example of a group of animals that was once very diverse,

862
01:18:37,024 --> 01:18:43,927
and all that's left today is a very small selection of that past diversity.

863
01:18:44,207 --> 01:18:49,129
And the example that I always go to, because it's so tempting to say, "Oh, well, this is what they're like today."

864
01:18:49,169 --> 01:18:49,389
Yeah.

865
01:18:49,409 --> 01:18:51,509
"This must be what they have been like."

866
01:18:51,589 --> 01:18:55,891
Well, obviously this works, so obviously it's what must have been happening.

867
01:18:56,071 --> 01:18:58,012
And that is when I point at Sloths.

868
01:18:58,092 --> 01:18:58,392
Yes.

869
01:18:58,892 --> 01:18:59,712
Episode 24.

870
01:19:00,322 --> 01:19:08,527
So, we still don't know a lot about exactly what role these genes play in the overall evolution of the fish,

871
01:19:09,328 --> 01:19:18,413
and what their entire function is, but there are some really cool connections between us and this weird fish.

872
01:19:18,513 --> 01:19:22,935
Yeah, some kinship. Some genetic kinship with this very strange fish.

873
01:19:23,146 --> 01:19:27,988
Now, to be fair, a Coelacanth would look at us and say, "You think I'm a strange fish?"

874
01:19:28,028 --> 01:19:29,129
Yes, exactly.

875
01:19:29,189 --> 01:19:29,889
My goodness.

876
01:19:30,949 --> 01:19:31,930
All of you up there.

877
01:19:31,950 --> 01:19:33,010
Good God, Matt.

878
01:19:33,430 --> 01:19:35,531
You are all the weirdest fish I've ever seen.

879
01:19:35,931 --> 01:19:38,792
But that wasn't the only weird stuff to come out of the genome sequence.

880
01:19:39,413 --> 01:19:47,516
They also found a weird trend in the genome, and this one is going to need some extra explanation,

881
01:19:47,526 --> 01:19:54,469
because what they found is it seems their genome evolves particularly slowly compared to other vertebrates.

882
01:19:54,609 --> 01:19:55,950
At least compared to mammals.

883
01:19:56,250 --> 01:19:57,230
Okay, I have heard this.

884
01:19:57,310 --> 01:20:01,052
Yes. Now, that phrase right there is a very dangerous phrase.

885
01:20:01,812 --> 01:20:02,232
Yes.

886
01:20:02,432 --> 01:20:05,313
Evolved slowly. What does that mean?

887
01:20:05,543 --> 01:20:14,048
What they mean by that is when they looked at the genome, they found that it had an unusually low rate of substitution,

888
01:20:14,088 --> 01:20:19,030
which is a type of mutation where sections of genes are just switched.

889
01:20:19,751 --> 01:20:19,931
Right.

890
01:20:20,191 --> 01:20:27,054
So not necessarily damaging, but just pieces being mixed up as it's replicated.

891
01:20:27,585 --> 01:20:30,806
Coelacanths have very little of that going on.

892
01:20:31,446 --> 01:20:35,788
And if you have low rates of mutation, that's going to slow down your rate of evolution.

893
01:20:36,268 --> 01:20:39,770
That means you're not changing as quickly as other groups.

894
01:20:40,650 --> 01:20:43,672
And coelacanths seem to be changing very slowly.

895
01:20:44,032 --> 01:20:51,835
Interesting. This is something that has been observed in other groups, that different groups of life have different rates of evolutionary change.

896
01:20:52,526 --> 01:20:56,708
So not unexpected, but notable that coelacanths seem to be slow at it.

897
01:20:57,408 --> 01:21:00,830
And there's a couple of reasons why this could be.

898
01:21:01,350 --> 01:21:08,753
They noticed that the genes have very little differentiation between the samples taken when sequenced,

899
01:21:09,533 --> 01:21:11,354
which could just be small population.

900
01:21:12,114 --> 01:21:12,334
Right.

901
01:21:12,494 --> 01:21:15,496
What it seems to be is the slow evolution instead.

902
01:21:16,324 --> 01:21:22,487
And, you know, this has been a question about coelacanths before we even did the sequence, because they look a lot like the fossil coelacanths.

903
01:21:22,827 --> 01:21:24,468
Right. Why are you so similar?

904
01:21:24,508 --> 01:21:31,671
Yeah. Is it is it because you're still evolving, but just not changing your shape or are you not evolving much?

905
01:21:31,691 --> 01:21:34,293
And it seems they actually aren't evolving much.

906
01:21:35,493 --> 01:21:40,116
And the answer for why that could be is not clear and cut. You know, it's it's evolution.

907
01:21:40,345 --> 01:21:42,766
It's always going to be difficult to. Yeah. And this is why.

908
01:21:43,546 --> 01:21:46,788
But there are a few reasons why these fish might be evolving more slowly.

909
01:21:47,428 --> 01:22:00,153
And part of it is that where at least the modern ones are living on the edge of volcanic islands and in the caves beneath them, their habitat's not changing very often, very quickly.

910
01:22:00,613 --> 01:22:02,354
It's a fairly stable habitat.

911
01:22:02,554 --> 01:22:03,615
Right. Low pressure.

912
01:22:03,763 --> 01:22:12,348
Low pressure. They are also a very low energy fish, which has few predators in that area.

913
01:22:12,608 --> 01:22:14,449
Right. Right. And that's what I mean by low pressure.

914
01:22:14,509 --> 01:22:16,610
I realize that talking about a deep sea animal.

915
01:22:16,650 --> 01:22:17,551
Yes, I know.

916
01:22:17,631 --> 01:22:18,291
That's confusing.

917
01:22:18,411 --> 01:22:20,352
Yeah. Low selective pressure.

918
01:22:20,573 --> 01:22:22,774
So the selective pressure on these fish.

919
01:22:22,794 --> 01:22:23,654
Pressure to change.

920
01:22:24,175 --> 01:22:25,535
Seems to be fairly low.

921
01:22:26,063 --> 01:22:31,346
They don't seem to have much reason evolutionarily to have to change often.

922
01:22:31,586 --> 01:22:32,566
Right. Not much impetus.

923
01:22:32,806 --> 01:22:33,066
Yes.

924
01:22:34,447 --> 01:22:40,490
Which brings us to one of the most notable things that the Coelacanth is famous for.

925
01:22:41,390 --> 01:22:45,653
They have become the poster child for a term known as the living fossil.

926
01:22:45,973 --> 01:22:49,674
Yes. This is like the quintessential example.

927
01:22:49,865 --> 01:22:54,867
If you go to the Wikipedia page for living fossils, the first picture is the Coelacanth.

928
01:22:55,148 --> 01:22:59,650
Yep. And if you are going back to the beginning of the episode, the Pokemon Relacanth.

929
01:22:59,930 --> 01:23:00,230
Yep.

930
01:23:00,570 --> 01:23:04,412
Its Pokedex entry says that it hasn't changed in millions and millions of years.

931
01:23:04,792 --> 01:23:10,935
Because that's what Coelacanth are famous for, is being quote unquote living fossils.

932
01:23:11,566 --> 01:23:16,948
And this term, though interesting, has some issues to it.

933
01:23:17,588 --> 01:23:21,110
And can often have some misunderstandings that it promotes.

934
01:23:21,290 --> 01:23:24,591
Right. A lot of paleontologists don't like this term.

935
01:23:24,751 --> 01:23:25,551
We number among them.

936
01:23:25,612 --> 01:23:25,852
Yes.

937
01:23:27,543 --> 01:23:37,748
Because it is a term that is often used in a way that doesn't quite match with the way we understand evolution scientifically.

938
01:23:38,148 --> 01:23:43,992
And the issue behind it is that it suggests, even if that's not what it means,

939
01:23:44,068 --> 01:23:54,632
because the official definition for a living fossil is a group that closely resembles a fossil member from its fossil record.

940
01:23:55,172 --> 01:23:57,973
Or a group that has shown very low rates of evolution.

941
01:23:58,474 --> 01:24:03,335
Now as far as official definition goes, it's not an official term.

942
01:24:03,384 --> 01:24:10,287
No, not like technical, but when it's used in more professional settings, this is what it's usually referring to.

943
01:24:10,367 --> 01:24:13,268
And that I believe was the original intent of the term.

944
01:24:13,889 --> 01:24:15,549
When it was coined by Charles Darwin.

945
01:24:15,569 --> 01:24:16,890
Charles Darwin.

946
01:24:16,970 --> 01:24:20,091
Which is the other reason that this term has a lot of...

947
01:24:20,371 --> 01:24:21,072
Clout.

948
01:24:21,092 --> 01:24:25,133
Yeah, this has some mileage on it and it's not slowing down.

949
01:24:25,304 --> 01:24:34,769
I believe he meant it as what you're saying basically, here is an animal that is a good proxy for us to make inferences about the ancient groups.

950
01:24:35,410 --> 01:24:39,212
Because it is similar to the ancient groups in many respects.

951
01:24:39,812 --> 01:24:48,277
And it was in kind of almost a throwaway line of organisms that show these features could almost be called living fossils.

952
01:24:48,337 --> 01:24:48,457
Right.

953
01:24:48,464 --> 01:24:51,104
Which I believe is almost the exact phrasing he used.

954
01:24:51,144 --> 01:24:51,865
Something like that.

955
01:24:52,045 --> 01:24:55,306
And so it was, he was saying like, yeah, you could call them that.

956
01:24:56,186 --> 01:24:57,947
He was not trying to define them as that.

957
01:24:57,967 --> 01:24:59,667
Right, he didn't mean to coin a term.

958
01:24:59,767 --> 01:25:00,507
Yeah, exactly.

959
01:25:00,927 --> 01:25:04,729
It's very much what happened with the survival of the fittest happening.

960
01:25:04,989 --> 01:25:07,830
Someone else summed it up that way and everyone went, that's catchy.

961
01:25:07,850 --> 01:25:08,870
Now that's how we say it.

962
01:25:10,124 --> 01:25:25,331
So this term is very popular, but it suggests that these animals have stepped out of evolution or have somehow like time capsuled their way to the modern day.

963
01:25:25,608 --> 01:25:39,593
Right, the way that it's commonly used, the way that a lot of people seem to interpret it is that this is an animal as a living fossil, meaning that it is literally the same thing as that 70 million year old fossil animal.

964
01:25:39,834 --> 01:25:41,714
That it's land of the lost style.

965
01:25:41,974 --> 01:25:44,055
Yeah, a land where time stood still.

966
01:25:44,215 --> 01:25:44,715
Exactly.

967
01:25:44,735 --> 01:25:48,717
This is the fish where time, this is the lava cave where time stood still.

968
01:25:49,624 --> 01:25:53,565
And none of that is remotely true.

969
01:25:53,906 --> 01:25:54,686
No, that's not how it works.

970
01:25:54,706 --> 01:25:56,307
That's not how it works.

971
01:25:56,887 --> 01:26:01,649
The one article I found had a really great tagline that says evolution leaves no fish behind.

972
01:26:03,150 --> 01:26:03,630
That's cute.

973
01:26:03,690 --> 01:26:04,750
Right?

974
01:26:05,110 --> 01:26:07,692
Well, I guess it leaves, actually it leaves a lot of fish behind.

975
01:26:07,772 --> 01:26:10,493
Yeah, well, I mean, it's only the ones that can't keep up.

976
01:26:10,786 --> 01:26:14,529
Right.

977
01:26:14,549 --> 01:26:18,132
But the point is that no, these fish have absolutely evolved.

978
01:26:18,407 --> 01:26:24,810
As we noted earlier, they're not even in the coelacanth group of the fossil fish.

979
01:26:25,410 --> 01:26:28,512
And they're living in a particularly odd place perhaps.

980
01:26:28,552 --> 01:26:28,892
Yeah.

981
01:26:29,132 --> 01:26:31,833
They have a different body shape and size.

982
01:26:32,503 --> 01:26:42,547
So though their genes do seem to be evolving more slowly, that doesn't mean they are a fossil equivalent swimming around.

983
01:26:42,867 --> 01:26:43,108
Right.

984
01:26:43,228 --> 01:26:44,348
They have changed.

985
01:26:44,408 --> 01:26:46,349
They are different from their ancestors.

986
01:26:46,729 --> 01:26:54,573
Though still superficially similar and even genetically perhaps more similar than many other animals would be.

987
01:26:54,826 --> 01:26:59,908
But there are even more clues showing us that no, this is still a dynamic group.

988
01:27:00,429 --> 01:27:09,353
So though most of the coelacanths we find are found right between Madagascar and Africa and over in that small area in the Indonesian ocean,

989
01:27:09,913 --> 01:27:17,296
there have been coelacanths found up and down the coast of South Africa, all the way up to Kenya.

990
01:27:17,648 --> 01:27:22,290
But usually these were just assumed to be strays that like got washed aside by a storm.

991
01:27:22,310 --> 01:27:23,110
This one's lost.

992
01:27:23,170 --> 01:27:23,851
Yeah, exactly.

993
01:27:23,871 --> 01:27:25,851
Like, you're not supposed to be here.

994
01:27:25,871 --> 01:27:26,972
What are you doing here, little buddy?

995
01:27:26,992 --> 01:27:28,713
Let me take you back to Madagascar.

996
01:27:28,733 --> 01:27:29,933
The coelacanths live down here.

997
01:27:31,114 --> 01:27:37,696
But since about 2003, close to a dozen coelacanths have been caught near Tanzania.

998
01:27:38,126 --> 01:27:44,129
And this kind of caught people's attention because there's no way that many can just all consistently be strays.

999
01:27:44,329 --> 01:27:44,549
Right.

1000
01:27:44,949 --> 01:27:46,130
So genetics.

1001
01:27:46,910 --> 01:27:51,752
Team did some more genetic testing, this time on mitochondrial genomes.

1002
01:27:51,772 --> 01:27:54,374
I think they sequenced the mitochondrial genome.

1003
01:27:54,774 --> 01:27:55,034
Neat.

1004
01:27:55,402 --> 01:28:02,705
And what they found is this Tanzanian population does carry distinct genetic markers.

1005
01:28:03,425 --> 01:28:06,126
That it seems to be a distinct population.

1006
01:28:06,186 --> 01:28:15,450
Maybe not a new species, but it is a unique population from the rest of the West Indian coelacanths we are aware of so far.

1007
01:28:16,086 --> 01:28:32,512
And depending on the references they use to try to date to the split of this new population, they may have been started to split as much as 200,000 to a few million years ago.

1008
01:28:33,492 --> 01:28:40,835
So the earlier studies were saying that the two modern species split many millions of years ago.

1009
01:28:41,409 --> 01:28:47,692
And this is a more recent potential split within the last maybe few million years or less.

1010
01:28:47,772 --> 01:28:52,813
That may be as recent as when the earliest humans were walking around.

1011
01:28:52,853 --> 01:28:56,995
So like the coelacanths are still diversifying.

1012
01:28:57,435 --> 01:28:58,815
They're still evolving.

1013
01:28:59,295 --> 01:29:01,756
They may even be still speciating.

1014
01:29:02,324 --> 01:29:09,527
So if this Tanzania population stays distinct long enough, we might have a third species of coelacanth.

1015
01:29:09,667 --> 01:29:11,048
Way, way, way in the future.

1016
01:29:11,068 --> 01:29:11,468
Someday.

1017
01:29:11,828 --> 01:29:16,250
But they are not stagnant creatures.

1018
01:29:16,270 --> 01:29:18,071
They're not in evolutionary stasis.

1019
01:29:18,224 --> 01:29:30,950
And that's the reason that most people that have an issue with living fossil aren't a fan of it, is because it diminishes how evolution behaves within the organism you're describing.

1020
01:29:31,510 --> 01:29:34,392
And it mischaracterizes that organism.

1021
01:29:34,572 --> 01:29:37,273
Especially because it's often overused.

1022
01:29:37,824 --> 01:29:46,989
Alligators and crocodiles are often called living fossils, even though none of the species we know today go back to the dinosaurs like everyone wants to say.

1023
01:29:48,310 --> 01:29:50,391
These are all fairly recent.

1024
01:29:50,931 --> 01:29:53,032
Not to say once again that they're evolving fast.

1025
01:29:53,472 --> 01:29:55,713
The American alligator has been around for 8 million years.

1026
01:29:56,774 --> 01:29:58,275
That's a long time for a species.

1027
01:29:58,944 --> 01:30:03,786
So there are species that evolve quicker and slower than others, but they're all evolving.

1028
01:30:04,687 --> 01:30:07,448
It's not just somehow they're avoiding it completely.

1029
01:30:07,468 --> 01:30:09,129
Because that's not really the way it works.

1030
01:30:09,269 --> 01:30:15,432
The coelacanth that we have today, the two of them are just as modern as any other species on the planet.

1031
01:30:15,552 --> 01:30:16,192
Exactly.

1032
01:30:16,773 --> 01:30:21,975
And before we wrap things up, I wanted to mention one other thing that the coelacanth has been known for.

1033
01:30:22,405 --> 01:30:28,067
Another group that has adopted them as their poster child are those who study cryptozoology.

1034
01:30:28,428 --> 01:30:29,488
Ooh, episode 46.

1035
01:30:30,288 --> 01:30:33,710
Those who believe that there are hidden creatures.

1036
01:30:34,030 --> 01:30:34,591
Cryptids.

1037
01:30:34,731 --> 01:30:35,311
Cryptids.

1038
01:30:35,391 --> 01:30:41,874
These mysterious, often viewed but never verified anomalies out in nature.

1039
01:30:42,084 --> 01:30:48,427
Right. Dinosaurs still living in Africa, plesiosaurs still living in Loch Ness in Scotland.

1040
01:30:48,547 --> 01:30:51,889
Great apes in the western United States.

1041
01:30:52,269 --> 01:31:05,895
These cryptozoologists often will point to the coelacanth as, if not proof, really good evidence for a lot of the claims of dinosaurs in Africa, plesiosaurs in lakes.

1042
01:31:06,268 --> 01:31:08,389
Because here we have an animal.

1043
01:31:08,449 --> 01:31:14,752
Here's a fish that we thought was extinct for tens of millions of years that then wasn't.

1044
01:31:15,112 --> 01:31:21,814
And so that's why this, the okapi, which was another one that was described before it was caught.

1045
01:31:22,895 --> 01:31:26,296
People said, "I've seen a thing that's half giraffe and half zebra."

1046
01:31:26,316 --> 01:31:27,617
And everyone went, "You're crazy."

1047
01:31:27,997 --> 01:31:29,617
And then years and years later, we found it.

1048
01:31:29,677 --> 01:31:30,078
Found it.

1049
01:31:30,802 --> 01:31:32,503
And went, "Oh wow, that's crazy."

1050
01:31:33,063 --> 01:31:33,563
I'm crazy.

1051
01:31:33,663 --> 01:31:34,323
I'm crazy.

1052
01:31:35,364 --> 01:31:45,928
This is an even more extreme example which has led lots of people to say, "Oh, I mean, if the coelacanth can do it, who says megalodon? Who says..."

1053
01:31:46,449 --> 01:31:47,089
Right, right, right.

1054
01:31:47,209 --> 01:31:48,449
Et cetera, et cetera, couldn't do it.

1055
01:31:49,463 --> 01:32:00,069
And the reason that that's not a one-to-one is this is a very unusual case and it's not a big animal.

1056
01:32:00,449 --> 01:32:11,975
Right. It does bring up the very interesting question of how could you end up with an animal that avoids the fossil record for 70 million years.

1057
01:32:12,049 --> 01:32:16,270
Yes, that's weird and notable. We need to look into that.

1058
01:32:16,750 --> 01:32:18,771
But we also don't have a lot of examples of that.

1059
01:32:19,351 --> 01:32:23,733
Right. Well, it's notable because it's so rare and it's so unusual.

1060
01:32:23,793 --> 01:32:24,353
Precisely.

1061
01:32:24,733 --> 01:32:26,394
And the coelacanth, like you're saying, it's small.

1062
01:32:26,674 --> 01:32:29,295
It's like six foot long that's a big fish.

1063
01:32:29,755 --> 01:32:29,995
Right.

1064
01:32:30,015 --> 01:32:35,717
But that's not... we're not as big as we like to think we are sometimes, though we are bigger than we often realize.

1065
01:32:36,104 --> 01:32:41,827
Well, and it's like you mentioned in your fish news, not all parts of the fish are good at fossilizing.

1066
01:32:42,867 --> 01:32:43,027
No.

1067
01:32:43,047 --> 01:32:50,210
And coelacanths also live in an environment that isn't particularly likely to leave fossils behind.

1068
01:32:50,250 --> 01:32:55,393
We don't find a lot of fossils on the sides of mountains, especially underwater.

1069
01:32:55,413 --> 01:33:01,235
Right, 800 feet underwater. You know, it's an uncommon place for them to become a fossil in the first place.

1070
01:33:01,745 --> 01:33:07,188
If the last 70 million years has been anything like today, they're also quite rare.

1071
01:33:07,428 --> 01:33:10,469
Yeah, and they have a very low impact on their ecosystem.

1072
01:33:10,769 --> 01:33:17,212
They aren't eating massive amounts. They're a low energy fish that is prey to very few animals.

1073
01:33:17,932 --> 01:33:26,475
You know, if they suddenly poofed, we wouldn't notice it on the surface by suddenly being like, oh my gosh, where are all of these predators disappearing to?

1074
01:33:26,507 --> 01:33:31,069
They must be starving because their food is... no, they're not the krill of the ocean.

1075
01:33:31,089 --> 01:33:35,931
They're one weirdo... two weirdos hanging out in two spots of the ocean.

1076
01:33:37,071 --> 01:33:46,075
So, yes, they are an example of what has been claimed for certain cryptids, but they're not actually as good an example as is often made out to be.

1077
01:33:46,355 --> 01:33:48,536
Right, they are an unusual case.

1078
01:33:48,656 --> 01:33:48,916
Yes.

1079
01:33:49,422 --> 01:33:55,224
It's also worth mentioning that when we say that they have avoided the fossil record for 70 million years, that is not the case.

1080
01:33:55,745 --> 01:33:58,066
They have avoided our notice in the fossil record.

1081
01:33:58,086 --> 01:34:00,367
Very, very true. That doesn't mean they haven't fossilized.

1082
01:34:00,867 --> 01:34:05,168
And there is something to be said, and I don't know very much about this aspect of it,

1083
01:34:05,449 --> 01:34:13,512
but I don't know how much fossil hunting is done in the areas that coelacancer found these days.

1084
01:34:14,246 --> 01:34:22,630
So even if they would be leaving fossil records, you know, like an animal that is a continental lake dweller in Europe

1085
01:34:23,030 --> 01:34:31,813
has a much higher likelihood of becoming fossilized and then found by people studying ancient lake sediments that are, you know, next door to their town or whatever.

1086
01:34:32,408 --> 01:34:42,511
But no, marine, deep marine fish in the Indian Ocean could also just be an area we're not doing a particularly good job of looking at

1087
01:34:42,631 --> 01:34:47,013
or that doesn't have a very easily accessible fossil record.

1088
01:34:47,153 --> 01:34:47,373
Yes.

1089
01:34:47,713 --> 01:34:49,034
The geology might not be there.

1090
01:34:49,582 --> 01:34:57,686
So coelacanths are an odd fish, which is part of why they were likely able to be the signature Lazarus taxon.

1091
01:34:57,746 --> 01:35:04,830
Exactly. So there's many good reasons for them to be notable, but some of these reasons have also caused them to get...

1092
01:35:05,190 --> 01:35:06,271
They've been overblown.

1093
01:35:06,511 --> 01:35:10,593
Yeah, and missed titles and, you know, fame.

1094
01:35:11,253 --> 01:35:13,574
Yeah, they're famous for things that they don't deserve.

1095
01:35:13,745 --> 01:35:20,448
Exactly. So they're a very odd group and we're still learning a lot about them because they're both protected.

1096
01:35:20,548 --> 01:35:23,210
You can't just go catch and take as many samples as you want.

1097
01:35:24,270 --> 01:35:34,615
It has to be very regimented and really only if they are caught and die by bycatch is usually how we're getting specimens.

1098
01:35:34,967 --> 01:35:40,370
Well, I can't wait until 20 years from now when we've been able to study them for one century.

1099
01:35:42,230 --> 01:35:42,450
Yep.

1100
01:35:42,731 --> 01:35:45,212
So we'll see what else we learn from them.

1101
01:35:45,652 --> 01:35:48,113
And that's going to wrap up our discussion of coelacanths.

1102
01:35:48,213 --> 01:35:51,554
But before we go, I want to talk about Patreon again.

1103
01:35:52,155 --> 01:35:52,255
Oh.

1104
