1108
01:36:04,848 --> 01:36:07,989
Our patron question today is from Jennifer Felix and Kale.

1109
01:36:08,529 --> 01:36:10,190
A family of patron supporters.

1110
01:36:10,250 --> 01:36:16,993
Yes, who say that they were looking at a book of Felix's about prehistoric life,

1111
01:36:17,385 --> 01:36:25,089
in which much of the paleo art shows the dinosaurs in deserts and flat, dry, cracked mud areas.

1112
01:36:25,429 --> 01:36:28,751
Felix wonders, is this unrealistic?

1113
01:36:29,151 --> 01:36:35,494
He feels this might not be a good representation, especially because there are no other life forms visible in the images.

1114
01:36:36,274 --> 01:36:38,395
So, how realistic are these environments?

1115
01:36:38,906 --> 01:36:43,728
Jennifer says that they know some fossils are commonly found in arid regions,

1116
01:36:44,128 --> 01:36:46,749
but do dinosaurs actually live in these type of environments?

1117
01:36:47,149 --> 01:36:49,530
That is an excellent question.

1118
01:36:49,631 --> 01:36:51,031
Yes, it's very observant to...

1119
01:36:51,051 --> 01:36:54,153
Felix, if I remember that right, is a young person.

1120
01:36:54,713 --> 01:36:59,835
Yes, many dinosaur fossils are found in the badlands and deserts and stuff,

1121
01:37:00,204 --> 01:37:06,126
but it is a great point to make, are those the environments that they actually lived in?

1122
01:37:06,607 --> 01:37:08,027
And in many cases, no.

1123
01:37:08,847 --> 01:37:18,011
A lot of cases of the badlands or the desert or whatever are areas that used to be forests or lakes or coastlines.

1124
01:37:18,391 --> 01:37:24,133
You know, we talked in episode 71 about how the center of North America was an ocean passage.

1125
01:37:24,153 --> 01:37:25,193
Yeah, it was underwater.

1126
01:37:25,765 --> 01:37:32,128
However, it's not unrealistic to depict dinosaurs living in places like that.

1127
01:37:32,768 --> 01:37:40,912
There are dinosaurs that are found in formations that represent ancient mudflats, tidal areas.

1128
01:37:41,249 --> 01:37:44,111
There are dinosaur fossils found from ancient deserts.

1129
01:37:44,511 --> 01:37:50,834
So, the Jadakhta Formation in Mongolia, which is where velociraptor and protoceratops and such are from,

1130
01:37:51,710 --> 01:37:56,792
is an ancient desert, so what the sediments we're looking through there, we, I don't do it,

1131
01:37:56,812 --> 01:38:01,054
but the sediments that paleontologists look through there are ancient dunes,

1132
01:38:01,774 --> 01:38:04,855
desert-type areas that had relatively little water.

1133
01:38:05,856 --> 01:38:09,337
Funnily enough, rather similar to what that area is like today.

1134
01:38:09,365 --> 01:38:10,445
Yeah, coincidentally.

1135
01:38:10,625 --> 01:38:13,327
Yeah, that's a case where, yeah, it is actually like that.

1136
01:38:14,007 --> 01:38:20,870
So, definitely there were dinosaurs in the desert, definitely there were dinosaurs on cracked, muddy ground.

1137
01:38:21,811 --> 01:38:22,851
Not all of them.

1138
01:38:23,772 --> 01:38:30,775
And depending on what dinosaur is in the picture, like if it's T-Rex in the desert, that's not the case.

1139
01:38:31,326 --> 01:38:36,048
T-Rex was the sort of forested marshy, I think, of the Hell Creek Formation.

1140
01:38:36,608 --> 01:38:39,870
Or at least, you know, lots of, a river floodplain.

1141
01:38:40,170 --> 01:38:43,752
But there were dinosaurs that did live in those kinds of habitats.

1142
01:38:44,292 --> 01:38:48,174
And I also like the point they made about there being no other life forms in the pictures.

1143
01:38:48,254 --> 01:38:49,434
Yeah, just that animal.

1144
01:38:49,535 --> 01:38:52,716
Yeah, which, to me, that's interesting on two points.

1145
01:38:53,076 --> 01:38:56,098
One, no, there were definitely other animals living alongside dinosaurs.

1146
01:38:56,865 --> 01:38:58,305
Right, other dinosaurs.

1147
01:38:58,365 --> 01:38:58,746
Yes.

1148
01:38:59,026 --> 01:39:02,147
And there were little early mammals and insects.

1149
01:39:02,187 --> 01:39:03,268
Reptiles, insects.

1150
01:39:03,308 --> 01:39:04,568
Small reptiles, all sorts of stuff.

1151
01:39:04,588 --> 01:39:06,169
Pterosaurs, all sorts of cool stuff.

1152
01:39:06,970 --> 01:39:13,573
But if you go out on a photo safari, you don't usually get a whole bunch of animals in one picture.

1153
01:39:14,413 --> 01:39:18,395
Because animals actually are, tend to like to have their own space from one another.

1154
01:39:19,155 --> 01:39:21,737
Especially because a lot of animals are food for other animals.

1155
01:39:21,764 --> 01:39:26,387
So being just out and about for no reason makes you a good target.

1156
01:39:26,587 --> 01:39:26,807
Yeah.

1157
01:39:27,067 --> 01:39:35,351
So it is very likely that if you could time travel back and take a photo safari, you probably would only get one or two animals in a picture if you saw any at all.

1158
01:39:35,831 --> 01:39:40,333
It's a fun trend that, it is sort of a broad trend in paleo art.

1159
01:39:40,646 --> 01:39:51,130
That if you look at some of the early paleo art works, you'll see these broad paintings with like as many species as they could cram into the image.

1160
01:39:51,150 --> 01:39:54,692
Yeah, like a dozen different kinds of animals and like two or three of each.

1161
01:39:54,952 --> 01:39:58,313
Right, all from that age or from that place.

1162
01:39:59,004 --> 01:40:09,247
And that's something that I feel like I see less of now, because modern artists are following the logic of, well, that's actually very unusual to see that many.

1163
01:40:09,387 --> 01:40:14,789
Yeah, unless they lived in a herd or a family group, most animals don't do that.

1164
01:40:14,949 --> 01:40:16,550
Right, Africa is a weird place.

1165
01:40:16,624 --> 01:40:28,348
Yes, and even in Africa, I've talked to people who have been back from Africa and done photo safaris, and there are whole days where you will drive around and not see anything.

1166
01:40:28,546 --> 01:40:43,671
Yeah, I've actually seen, I don't remember where I've seen this, but I know at one point I saw an artist who did a series of paleo art reconstructions, which they were trying to make the point of them being more realistic.

1167
01:40:44,030 --> 01:40:58,155
So it'd be like a scene of a forest, and then like way in the distance off in the corner of the image, half hidden behind a tree, you'd see like the back end of a triceratops.

1168
01:40:58,995 --> 01:41:07,638
They're actually really hard to find, and if you went looking for dinosaurs in the Mesozoic, this might be the best shot you get.

1169
01:41:07,949 --> 01:41:10,170
It reminds me, I went to Yellowstone many years ago.

1170
01:41:10,190 --> 01:41:12,070
Yes, that's exactly what I was thinking of.

1171
01:41:12,390 --> 01:41:14,971
And I saw the butt of a grizzly bear.

1172
01:41:15,011 --> 01:41:15,951
Yeah.

1173
01:41:16,512 --> 01:41:21,053
I have seen a grizzly in person, only its rear end, and that's all I got.

